---
/*
 * /code-signing : how the Windows builds get signed. The footer links here on
 * every page. The publisher name must match AZURE_SIGNING_PUBLISHER in the
 * grimoire repo's Actions variables.
 */
import Site from '../layouts/Site.astro';
import { brand } from '../data/content';

const EFFECTIVE = '26 October 2026';
const maintainer = 'https://github.com/Slush97';
---

<Site title="Code Signing Policy · Grimoire" description="How the Windows builds of Grimoire get signed, who can sign one, and how to check a signature.">
  <article class="wrap policy prose">
    <p class="kicker">Effective {EFFECTIVE}</p>
    <h1>Code signing policy</h1>

    <p>
      Windows builds are signed with Microsoft's Azure Artifact Signing, starting with v1.34.0. The
      publisher is <strong>Eliott Humes</strong>, the maintainer. Older releases are unsigned.
    </p>

    <h2>What gets signed</h2>
    <p>
      The installer (<code>Grimoire-Setup-&lt;version&gt;.exe</code>), the portable
      <code>Grimoire-Portable-&lt;version&gt;.exe</code>, and every <code>.exe</code> inside them.
      GitHub Actions builds them from <a href={brand.github}>Slush97/grimoire</a> and signs them in
      the same run, for releases and nightlies. The build fails if any of them comes out unsigned.
      Nothing built on someone's own machine gets signed.
    </p>
    <p>
      The installer also carries <code>vpkmerge.exe</code>, the open source tool Grimoire uses to
      read and write game files. GitHub Actions builds it from
      <a href="https://github.com/Slush97/vpkmerge">Slush97/vpkmerge</a>.
    </p>
    <p>
      Linux and macOS builds aren't covered here. Every release ships a <code>SHA256SUMS</code>
      file and build provenance attestations for all platforms, and the
      <a href="/download">download page</a> shows how to check them.
    </p>

    <h2>Who can sign</h2>
    <p>
      Grimoire has one maintainer, <a href={maintainer}>Slush97</a>, the only account that can merge
      to <code>main</code> or tag a release. Changes from anyone else get reviewed before they're
      merged. The signing credentials live in the repo's Actions secrets, and only the Windows build
      job can read them.
    </p>

    <h2>Privacy</h2>
    <p>
      Grimoire has no telemetry. Every request the app can make, who receives it, and how to turn it
      off is on <a href="/docs/privacy">what leaves your machine</a>, along with links to the
      privacy policies of the services it talks to. This website has
      <a href="/privacy">its own privacy policy</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>Checking a signature</h2>
    <p>
      Right-click the <code>.exe</code>, open <strong>Properties</strong>, then the
      <strong>Digital Signatures</strong> tab. It should list Eliott Humes. In PowerShell,
      <code>Get-AuthenticodeSignature &lt;file&gt;</code> should print <code>Valid</code> and
      <code>CN=Eliott Humes</code>.
    </p>
    <p>
      SmartScreen can still warn about a new release for a while. If the warning names Eliott Humes
      as the publisher, click <strong>More info</strong>, then <strong>Run anyway</strong>. If it
      says unknown publisher on v1.34.0 or newer, don't run it.
    </p>

    <h2>Something looks wrong?</h2>
    <p>
      If a signed file doesn't match a release, or you think this policy was broken, open an issue
      on <a href={`${brand.github}/issues`}>GitHub</a> or say so in the
      <a href={brand.discord}>Discord</a>.
    </p>
  </article>
</Site>

<style>
  .policy {
    max-width: 808px;
    padding-top: 72px;
    padding-bottom: 120px;
  }
  .policy h1 {
    font-size: 64px;
  }
  @media (max-width: 719px) {
    .policy {
      padding-top: 48px;
      padding-bottom: 80px;
    }
    .policy h1 {
      font-size: 48px;
    }
  }
</style>
